; lambda calculus
;
; covers: lambda abstraction (`λx. body`, written `λ $x %B`) and beta-reduction --
; `(λ $x %B) $a = sub $x $a %B`, reusing kurt's own built-in `sub` substitution operator
; (the same one `forall-elim`/`exists-intro` use) rather than defining a new one from
; scratch. Application itself needs no new syntax at all: `(λ $x %B) $a` is just ordinary
; space-juxtaposition, exactly like `set.kurt`'s `$f $a` for function application.
; Alpha-equivalence is free too -- two lambda terms that differ only in their bound
; variable's name already compare equal via kurt's own bound-variable-aware matching
; (see doc/kurt-soundness.md #3.3), no extra axiom needed.
;
; known gaps, all stemming from the same root cause -- `sub`'s schema-decomposition
; matching (the mechanism that lets `sub $x $a %A` match a concrete goal by finding what
; `%A` must have been) only fires when the thing substituted into is boolean-typed
; (`match_against_sub` in kurt.py requires `kb.is_bool` on that schema variable) -- so this
; is really a *predicate* lambda calculus (lambda terms whose bodies are propositions about
; their argument), not the fully general, arbitrary-valued untyped lambda calculus:
;   - a lambda whose body is a compound expression mentioning the bound variable (e.g.
;     `λx. P(x)`, `λx. P(x) and Q(x)`) beta-reduces fine, see proofs/lambda-calculus/.
;   - a lambda whose body is *exactly* the bound variable and nothing else (the identity
;     function, `λx. x`) does not beta-reduce via this axiom -- the decomposition mechanism
;     doesn't find that degenerate a "hole". Not the same issue as the general
;     equal-elim/flat-operator matching gap (doc/kurt-soundness.md #6), but the same
;     flavor: a real, un-chased completeness limitation, not unsoundness.
;   - currying (a lambda whose body is *itself* another lambda, e.g. `λx. λy. P(x,y)`) does
;     not beta-reduce either, even with the inner lambda's own body boolean -- the
;     decomposition mechanism can't find a "hole" that itself contains a binder. So every
;     lambda here is single-argument; there's no built-up multi-argument application.
;   - no eta-conversion (`λx. (f x) = f`, provided `x` doesn't occur free in `f`) -- kurt's
;     axiom syntax has no way to state that side condition, and omitting it would make the
;     axiom unsound (nothing would stop `f` from being instantiated to something that does
;     mention `x`).
load equality

;; syntax: `λ` binds a variable over a boolean body, exactly like `forall`/`exists` bind a
;; variable over a boolean condition -- the difference is `λx. B` is a *term* (an applicable
;; value), not itself a proposition, so unlike `forall`/`exists` its own `bool` declaration
;; only marks its *body* (position 2) boolean, not the whole expression... except position 0
;; has to be marked boolean too (`bool lambda 0 2`), purely so a lambda whose own body is
;; another lambda can still be substituted via `sub`'s boolean-only decomposition -- ONE OF
;; the effects of this (not a further gap on top of the ones above) is what makes nested
;; lambdas fail to *beta-reduce* even though they still parse and type-check fine.
arity lambda 2
bindop lambda
bool lambda 0 2
alias λ lambda

;; beta-reduction: applying a lambda abstraction to an argument substitutes the argument for
;; the bound variable in the body -- the one computational rule of lambda calculus
use (λ $x %B) $a = sub $x $a %B    "beta-reduction"

; for automatic testing we add the following line:
